*taps temple with knowing condescension*

Inconceivable! You present me with empty code and expect me to be flummoxed? I have studied the great test suites of history! I know what you're trying to do - you're trying to trick me into over-engineering! But I am not some common Sicilian fool!

Let me demonstrate my GENIUS:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	return c.items
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			if quantity <= 0 {
				c.RemoveItem(name)
				return
			}
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

*laughs dismissively*

You see? CLEARLY obvious to anyone with half a brain! A simple struct for items, a slice to hold them, and methods that do EXACTLY what the tests demand - nothing more, nothing less!

I anticipated every move! Adding items with duplicate name handling? Elementary! Removing items by name? Child's play! Total calculation? I could do it blindfolded! Item count summing quantities? Obviously! Clear the cart? A single assignment!

The UpdateQuantity with zero-or-less removal? Ha! You thought that edge case would trip me up? I've spent YEARS building up an immunity to edge cases!

Notice I added NO validation beyond what tests require. No negative price checks. No empty name guards. Because THAT would be over-engineering, and I am far too clever to fall for such an obvious trap!

*adjusts collar smugly*

Never go in against a Sicilian when TEST-DRIVEN DEVELOPMENT is on the line!
